{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J793TTe7oxcAiBWcMmexfk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental measurement temperature dependence cm2/s\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Structural and transport properties of LiTFSI/G3 electrolyte with machine-learned molecular dynamics\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.20243\"},{\"title\":\"Local Structure Dictates Ionic Transport and Mechanical Properties in Glassy Solid Electrolytes for Lithium Batteries\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06895\"},{\"title\":\"m.math-net.ru\",\"url\":\"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/ftt11863\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion in liquid lithium from coherent quasielastic neutron scattering\",\"url\":\"https://katalog.lib.cas.cz/KNAV/EdsRecord/edsair,edsair.doi...........2430b859cd2c8ede718b24c249f43fd0\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553\"},{\"title\":\"peeref.com\",\"url\":\"https://peeref.com/works/14514882\"},{\"title\":\"Fizika Tverdogo Tela\",\"url\":\"https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/ftt11863\"},{\"title\":\"m.math-net.ru\",\"url\":\"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/tvt693\"}]\n\nThe search turned up several experimental and simulation studies on liquid lithium self-diffusion, but none of the results gave the actual numerical coefficients in cm²/s, so I can't report values from these snippets.\n\n- **Blagoveshchenskii, Novikov and Savostin (2012/2014):** Their quasi-elastic neutron scattering work covers the 500–830 K range and reports the temperature dependence of liquid lithium's self-diffusion. They compared the coherent scattering results with analogous data extracted by the incoherent scattering method. The 2014 follow-up in *Fizika Tverdogo Tela* is titled \"Self-diffusion in liquid lithium and lead from coherent quasi-elastic neutron scattering data\" (it also reports one temperature point, 623 K, for liquid lead).\n- **Murday and Cotts (1968):** This is the classic reference for the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient, cited in later work. Earlier experimental datasets include Feinauer et al. (1994) on ⁶Li and ⁷Li diffusivities and Ominia's isotope studies (1986, 2006), as listed in the references of a 2012 paper.\n- **Molecular dynamics (Wang and Ni, 2012):** An EAM-potential study reports that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature and decreases with pressure. The same paper says that little experimental self-diffusion data exist for liquid lithium because radiation-based measurement methods carry higher risk.\n- **Belashchenko and Ostrovskii (1995/2009):** Their EAM molecular dynamics study reports that under isobaric heating, the self-diffusion coefficients rise with temperature following a power law with an exponent of about 1.72.\n\nFor the actual cm²/s values and the Arrhenius fit parameters, the Murday–Cotts (1968, *J. Chem. Phys.* 48, 4938) and Blagoveshchenskii (2014) papers are the primary sources to check directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a3ee98b-7002-4283-82d0-8dda5402a44f","uuid":"1338c828-9240-4d72-a7f6-e4f96b2c69b2","timestamp":"2026-11-01T03:13:55.276Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental measurement temperature dependence cm2/s","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01SWASHq3TPaYUkRovGxiv2E","content":[{"title":"Structural and transport properties of LiTFSI/G3 electrolyte with machine-learned molecular dynamics","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.20243"},{"title":"Local Structure Dictates Ionic Transport and Mechanical Properties in Glassy Solid Electrolytes for Lithium Batteries","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.06895"},{"title":"m.math-net.ru","url":"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/ftt11863"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"Self-diffusion in liquid lithium from coherent quasielastic neutron scattering","url":"https://katalog.lib.cas.cz/KNAV/EdsRecord/edsair,edsair.doi...........2430b859cd2c8ede718b24c249f43fd0"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553"},{"title":"peeref.com","url":"https://peeref.com/works/14514882"},{"title":"Fizika Tverdogo Tela","url":"https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/ftt11863"},{"title":"m.math-net.ru","url":"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/tvt693"}]},"The search turned up several experimental and simulation studies on liquid lithium self-diffusion, but none of the results gave the actual numerical coefficients in cm²/s, so I can't report values from these snippets.\n\n- **Blagoveshchenskii, Novikov and Savostin (2012/2014):** Their quasi-elastic neutron scattering work covers the 500–830 K range and reports the temperature dependence of liquid lithium's self-diffusion. They compared the coherent scattering results with analogous data extracted by the incoherent scattering method. The 2014 follow-up in *Fizika Tverdogo Tela* is titled \"Self-diffusion in liquid lithium and lead from coherent quasi-elastic neutron scattering data\" (it also reports one temperature point, 623 K, for liquid lead).\n- **Murday and Cotts (1968):** This is the classic reference for the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient, cited in later work. Earlier experimental datasets include Feinauer et al. (1994) on ⁶Li and ⁷Li diffusivities and Ominia's isotope studies (1986, 2006), as listed in the references of a 2012 paper.\n- **Molecular dynamics (Wang and Ni, 2012):** An EAM-potential study reports that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature and decreases with pressure. The same paper says that little experimental self-diffusion data exist for liquid lithium because radiation-based measurement methods carry higher risk.\n- **Belashchenko and Ostrovskii (1995/2009):** Their EAM molecular dynamics study reports that under isobaric heating, the self-diffusion coefficients rise with temperature following a power law with an exponent of about 1.72.\n\nFor the actual cm²/s values and the Arrhenius fit parameters, the Murday–Cotts (1968, *J. Chem. Phys.* 48, 4938) and Blagoveshchenskii (2014) papers are the primary sources to check directly."],"durationSeconds":5.559649227000016,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01J793TTe7oxcAiBWcMmexfk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
